## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, $A'$ and $B'$ fill $S_{0,4}$.

**Definition.** Two closed curves $A, B$ on a surface $S$ *fill* $S$ if the complement $S \setminus (A \cup B)$ is a disjoint union of topological disks (and, for surfaces with boundary, possibly collar neighborhoods of boundary components — in any case, regions that are simply connected).

**Key fact.** The mapping class group $\text{MCG}(S_{0,4})$ is the group of orientation-preserving self-homeomorphisms of $S_{0,4}$ (fixing $\partial S_{0,4}$ pointwise) modulo isotopy. Every element $\phi \in \text{MCG}(S_{0,4})$ is represented by an actual homeomorphism $f: S_{0,4} \to S_{0,4}$.

**Argument.** Let $f: S_{0,4} \to S_{0,4}$ be a homeomorphism representing $\phi$. Since $A$ and $B$ are closed curves, their images $f(A)$ and $f(B)$ are closed curves (homeomorphisms map closed curves to closed curves). These images are precisely $A' = \phi(A)$ and $B' = \phi(B)$ (up to isotopy, which does not affect filling).

Now consider the complement. The restriction of $f$ to the open set $S_{0,4} \setminus (A \cup B)$ is a homeomorphism onto its image:

$$f\big|_{S \setminus (A \cup B)} : S_{0,4} \setminus (A \cup B) \;\xrightarrow{\;\cong\;}\; S_{0,4} \setminus (A' \cup B').$$

This is a homeomorphism because $f$ is a global homeomorphism and $f(A \cup B) = A' \cup B'$, so the complement maps bijectively and continuously (with continuous inverse) onto the complement.

Since $A$ and $B$ fill $S_{0,4}$, the complement $S_{0,4} \setminus (A \cup B)$ is a disjoint union of disks. A homeomorphism maps a disk to a disk (homeomorphisms preserve all topological properties, including being simply connected and being a 2-manifold with the appropriate boundary structure). Therefore:

$$S_{0,4} \setminus (A' \cup B') = f\big(S_{0,4} \setminus (A \cup B)\big)$$

is also a disjoint union of disks. Hence $A'$ and $B'$ fill $S_{0,4}$.

**Conclusion.** Filling is a topological property of the pair $(S, A \cup B)$, preserved under any self-homeomorphism of $S$. Since every mapping class group element is represented by a homeomorphism, the image of a filling pair under any $\phi \in \text{MCG}(S_{0,4})$ is again a filling pair. $\blacksquare$

